00:31:46,101 --> 00:31:47,925
Scott: Let us meet the

360
00:31:47,945 --> 00:31:48,245
Scott: moment.".

361
00:31:48,446 --> 00:31:51,091
Scott: And we met the moment, and I played Friends vs.

362
00:31:51,111 --> 00:31:54,578
Scott: Friends, and now, more friends have come to play Friends vs.

363
00:31:54,598 --> 00:31:55,660
Scott: Friends, and we're getting our money's worth.

364
00:31:55,940 --> 00:31:56,960
Scott: We're getting more than our money's worth.

365
00:31:57,000 --> 00:32:02,759
Rym: Yeah, well, because I saw you two talk about it on two non-king-secutive occasions, so I was like, "I'm gonna play it right

366
00:32:02,799 --> 00:32:02,960
Rym: now.".

367
00:32:03,140 --> 00:32:10,960
Rym: And I just played it, and then I got one of our other friends to play it, and now we've got enough people to get, like, a 3v3 going, no problem, just in, like, our one Discord.

368
00:32:11,702 --> 00:32:12,824
Scott: So here's how the game works.

369
00:32:13,044 --> 00:32:17,354
Scott: So you buy the game for a couple bucks, and you join a battle.

370
00:32:17,915 --> 00:32:19,017
Scott: Well, first you gotta make a deck.

371
00:32:19,057 --> 00:32:20,220
Scott: You get a deck to start with.

372
00:32:20,440 --> 00:32:27,712
Scott: And you can, as you play, you're gonna get more currency, and you can open packs, and all this stuff, but you can't spend any money to do this.

373
00:32:27,852 --> 00:32:29,235
Scott: It's pure grind.

374
00:32:29,956 --> 00:32:32,320
Scott: It's grind only to upgrade your deck.

375
00:32:32,700 --> 00:32:34,727
Rym: And it's fairly automatic grind.

376
00:32:34,807 --> 00:32:37,195
Rym: Just playing will get you everything over time.

377
00:32:37,235 --> 00:32:38,720
Rym: You can get it a little faster.

378
00:32:39,480 --> 00:32:46,000
Scott: So I mean, you are at somewhat of a disadvantage to people who've been playing longer, but you're at a skill disadvantage to them anyway.

379
00:32:46,861 --> 00:32:55,500
Scott: Because even if we swapped accounts, and I got an account that was mega old and had every card and fully leveled up, I would lose to someone with a brand new account who knew what the fuck they were doing.

380
00:32:55,520 --> 00:32:58,651
Rym: Yeah, no, Penny fucking steamrolled me.

381
00:32:59,393 --> 00:33:01,159
Rym: And it wasn't cards, it was headshots.

382
00:33:03,501 --> 00:33:03,846
Scott: Exactly.

383
00:33:04,129 --> 00:33:05,000
Scott: I'm bad at the headshots.

384
00:33:05,560 --> 00:33:10,140
Rym: I got better at the headshots, and it made me better at Overwatch headshots.

385
00:33:10,700 --> 00:33:14,792
Scott: Right, but you build a deck, and you spawn, and you have a weapon.

386
00:33:14,812 --> 00:33:17,500
Scott: There's melee weapons and ranged weapons of various kinds.

387
00:33:18,060 --> 00:33:22,732
Scott: And you can flip through the cards in your hand left or right, and then you can use cards.

388
00:33:23,313 --> 00:33:25,880
Scott: And you use a card, and the card does what it says.

389
00:33:26,241 --> 00:33:36,419
Scott: Some of the cards are boring, like, "Give me more health," or, "Temporarily put on some armor," or, "Make my head small for a little while so it's hard to hit me in the

390
00:33:36,459 --> 00:33:36,680
Scott: head.".

391
00:33:36,860 --> 00:33:39,546
Scott: Things of that nature that are kind of obvious for an FPS.

392
00:33:39,606 --> 00:33:45,800
Scott: Some cards drop this weapon on the ground, so I now have a different, better weapon than the one I came with.

393
00:33:45,820 --> 00:33:48,063
Scott: There's no weapons lying around the levels, right?

394
00:33:49,345 --> 00:33:51,627
Scott: But now you got the interesting cards.

395
00:33:51,768 --> 00:33:56,413
Scott: There's one that's like, "Alright, cover me in an invincible shield of ice.

396
00:33:56,794 --> 00:34:01,500
Scott: I can't move, I can see, I can't shoot, but at least I'll live a little longer.

397
00:34:01,860 --> 00:34:08,359
Scott: And so if a teammate comes or my opponent runs out of, has to reload or something, I'll get a chance, and I won't die right at this

398
00:34:08,418 --> 00:34:08,679
Scott: moment.".

399
00:34:08,940 --> 00:34:12,645
Rym: Or, "Drop a turret, and now you've just got a turret that's fighting with

400
00:34:12,665 --> 00:34:12,824
Rym: you.".

401
00:34:13,226 --> 00:34:15,248
Rym: Or, "Hot potato, play this

402
00:34:15,308 --> 00:34:15,649
Rym: card.".

403
00:34:15,829 --> 00:34:17,170
Rym: It appears in the other person's hand.

404
00:34:17,511 --> 00:34:24,520
Rym: If they don't play the card in the next five seconds, it blows up, and then they play it back to you, and then you've got five seconds, and this just goes on until someone bites you.

405
00:34:24,520 --> 00:34:27,004
Scott: But there's a card that's like, "Alright, use this card.

406
00:34:27,143 --> 00:34:29,288
Scott: Now, whenever you use a card, it throws a

407
00:34:29,328 --> 00:34:29,728
Scott: bomb.".

408
00:34:29,809 --> 00:34:33,554
Scott: And so you fill your hand, you got another card that's like a lottery card.

409
00:34:33,594 --> 00:34:36,760
Scott: that's like one in a zillion chance for something good to happen.

410
00:34:37,199 --> 00:34:42,150
Scott: But if something bad happens, your hand fills with garbage cards, and you have to use the garbage cards to get rid of them.

411
00:34:42,531 --> 00:34:47,159
Scott: But if you got the bomb card, all those garbage cards are now bombs that you can throw.

412
00:34:48,340 --> 00:34:59,990
Rym: The game's just natively fun, and the cards are natively fun, but you can make combos with the cards, and it's not 100% deterministic that you get to do what you wanted, because it's like magic.

413
00:35:00,030 --> 00:35:13,120
Rym: There's a minimum deck size, and there's also, what we called it when I was playing with other people while you were away, a maximum tonnage, like an old mech warrior, because you get to use a maximum number of points and a minimum number of cards.

414
00:35:13,541 --> 00:35:24,300
Rym: You can have more cards, but there's a limit on how powerful all the cards in your deck can be, and you draw the cards over the course of the match, so it's not like you're always gonna get your combo.

415
00:35:25,180 --> 00:35:27,905
Scott: In the first round, you draw a few cards randomly.

416
00:35:27,925 --> 00:35:32,733
Scott: You can't just pick which ones from your deck you're gonna get, so you only have access to the ones you've drawn.

417
00:35:33,154 --> 00:35:36,500
Scott: If you don't use some, you'll still have them in the next round.

418
00:35:37,141 --> 00:35:45,080
Scott: So you can try to get your combo that way, but if you've got one card in the combo, don't use it until you get the other card, and then you'll get a win during some round.

419
00:35:45,240 --> 00:35:48,185
Rym: Or you make the calculus of like, "Do I think I can win this one?

420
00:35:48,406 --> 00:35:51,511
Rym: Should I use another card to push it over the line, or am I fucked?

421
00:35:51,711 --> 00:35:56,199
Rym: Should I just save my cards, try to win if I can, and just win the next

422
00:35:56,259 --> 00:35:56,520
Rym: round?".

423
00:35:57,360 --> 00:35:57,881
Scott: Exactly.

424
00:35:57,961 --> 00:36:00,745
Scott: So, there's so many interesting things you can do.

425
00:36:00,785 --> 00:36:11,360
Scott: The most basic thing I did, which is just really straightforward and easy, was I had a card, I think you'll get it when you start the game, that's like, "Freeze your opponent's weapon for a limited

426
00:36:11,400 --> 00:36:11,500
Scott: time.".

427
00:36:11,500 --> 00:36:12,258
Rym: Oh, that one's so good.

428
00:36:12,960 --> 00:36:13,702
Scott: So they can't shoot.

429
00:36:14,143 --> 00:36:19,474
Scott: And it's like, "Oh, I'll just hide, and then when they get close, ah!

430
00:36:19,714 --> 00:36:19,935
Scott: I can

431
00:36:19,975 --> 00:36:20,376
Scott: freeze!".

432
00:36:20,516 --> 00:36:22,240
Scott: And it's like, I don't have to aim that shit.

433
00:36:22,821 --> 00:36:30,110
Rym: But then you combo that with another card, bullet time, slows everybody down, and for like six seconds, the game is just in super slow motion.

434
00:36:30,431 --> 00:36:37,360
Rym: So you do 'em both, now they can't shoot you, you can shoot them, and everyone's moving in slow motion, I hope you can click on that head.

435
00:36:38,221 --> 00:36:48,476
Scott: The other card that worked very well for me was the ninja log, which is basically, it's basically when the next attack hits you, it doesn't hit you.

436
00:36:48,576 --> 00:36:51,000
Scott: Instead, a log appears like fucking Naruto.

437
00:36:52,800 --> 00:37:02,557
Scott: And you didn't get hit by that first attack, so this really helps with my poor reaction time of old man hand, because someone shoots me first, and now, "Haha!

438
00:37:02,697 --> 00:37:03,979
Scott: I'm shooting at them and they're shooting at

439
00:37:04,020 --> 00:37:04,100
Scott: me!".

440
00:37:04,300 --> 00:37:10,769
Scott: And on sort of equal timing, because their advantage of seeing me first has now been eliminated.

441
00:37:12,030 --> 00:37:19,180
Scott: And now it's just gonna come down to, I guess, the accuracy of the following shots, rather than me losing because I got shot first.

442
00:37:19,443 --> 00:37:20,778
Scott: The ninja log protected me.

443
00:37:21,461 --> 00:37:25,640
Rym: The primary gameplay beyond the cards, because the cards are real fun, like they make the game.

444
00:37:25,820 --> 00:37:28,020
Scott: The cards are almost half the game.

445
00:37:28,260 --> 00:37:43,715
Rym: Yeah, but the game fundamentally is a competitive first person shooter, like duels, like best of five, best of three matches, where you're just trying to kill the other people, shoot 'em in the head, sneak it around the map, there's a bunch of different maps, the maps are all

446
00:37:44,296 --> 00:37:44,976
Scott: wildly

447
00:37:45,016 --> 00:37:48,640
Rym: different in what's going on, they all got fun stuff to interact with.

448
00:37:48,680 --> 00:37:58,280
Rym: Like every map, like one of them is a bunch of cars driving down the highway, one of them is a subway station, one of them is like a theater with a bunch of stuff in it.

449
00:37:59,641 --> 00:38:10,700
Rym: I really like the one that has the four colors, the reality one, like the maze with the four color areas, because if you look up, there's a jumbotron that shows a camera pointed at the other areas, so people can't hide from you that easily.

450
00:38:11,641 --> 00:38:15,819
Scott: I like the one with the swimming pools, because the water can do lots of fun, unexpected things.

451
00:38:17,060 --> 00:38:20,820
Scott: There's also the one that's just like a tiny island with a mountain in the middle of it, and that's it.

452
00:38:20,960 --> 00:38:23,440
Rym: I love to jump up on top of the mountain and just throw a bunch of turrets everywhere.

453
00:38:23,940 --> 00:38:24,040
Rym: Right,

454
00:38:24,100 --> 00:38:35,840
Scott: it's like do you get up on top of the mountain or do you go around the side, it's like the advantage of height, but then you can be seen from all sides, whereas if you're on only one side, they can only see you if they're at the top or on the same side as you.

455
00:38:36,660 --> 00:38:38,920
Scott: And you still have the water thing going on as well.

456
00:38:38,940 --> 00:38:49,720
Rym: Three times, like I thought it would have been fine, they play like, first she's got the, I move slower, and then she's moving faster, and then she's got like a katana, so I'm just fucked, like I can't get away.

457
00:38:52,461 --> 00:38:59,920
Rym: The matches are crazy quick, like a couple minutes unless it's like a really tense, long, sneaky standoff.

458
00:39:00,521 --> 00:39:15,000
Scott: So even when I go and play against randos online and just get completely slaughtered every single match, they're so fast that it really doesn't bother me so much, and as long as I do something, I'm still earning and upgrading my cards and my deck, and getting new cards.

459
00:39:15,360 --> 00:39:27,040
Scott: So it's like okay, I'm just sort of learning the game, practicing, losing a whole bunch, because I terrible, but I'm upgrading my deck and practicing, which hopefully I will improve slowly over time.

460
00:39:27,400 --> 00:39:34,070
Rym: So the game distills some of the FPS stuff too, that I appreciate, in that you're either running or crouching.

461
00:39:34,591 --> 00:39:36,514
Rym: If you're running, you make footstep noises.

462
00:39:36,914 --> 00:39:40,700
Rym: If you're crouching, you move a lot slower, and you don't make footstep noises.

463
00:39:40,760 --> 00:39:47,280
Rym: They just got rid of that like walk in the middle that a lot of FPSs have had forever because it doesn't add anything to the game.

464
00:39:47,621 --> 00:39:54,300
Rym: Like every aspect is distilled to you are playing a fast, dual FPS with cards with your friends.

465
00:39:55,861 --> 00:40:10,100
Rym: And what it evokes, the thing that I think I like about this game the most, is that it feels a lot like how it felt when I was in high school, and I would go to a weird Quake server with some of my friends, and we'd play weird mods against each other.

466
00:40:10,400 --> 00:40:11,843
Rym: Like it feels like that.

467
00:40:14,527 --> 00:40:21,900
Rym: And the cards sort of bring in the randomness of modding and nonsense, and it lets nonsense happen, so it isn't just a game of clicking on the heads.

468
00:40:22,240 --> 00:40:27,630
Rym: But at the same time, if you're better at FPSs, you will win a lot more often.

469
00:40:27,650 --> 00:40:33,540
Rym: FPS skills are 80% of the game, card management is 20% in terms of actually winning.

470
00:40:36,221 --> 00:40:41,120
Scott: So let's talk about some things other than that are in the game but aren't part of the game.

471
00:40:41,380 --> 00:40:46,300
Scott: So there's a lobby in the game, which disappointingly is only a solo lobby.

472
00:40:48,361 --> 00:40:48,922
Scott: It's basically like.

473
00:40:48,942 --> 00:40:59,120
Scott: you can just use the menus to customize your deck and do all that stuff, or you can walk around this little area which is sort of a strip mall with a laundromat and a diner and various businesses.

474
00:40:59,360 --> 00:41:01,980
Rym: A few little hidden things to find, like some characters.

475
00:41:02,822 --> 00:41:06,620
Scott: All the characters of the game are there, you can switch characters this way.

476
00:41:07,140 --> 00:41:09,686
Scott: And it's like this really fun environment to hang out in.

477
00:41:10,067 --> 00:41:15,300
Scott: They don't let you hang out in it with other people, even when you're on a team and you're going to queue together.

478
00:41:15,681 --> 00:41:16,865
Scott: That's really disappointing.

479
00:41:16,905 --> 00:41:19,415
Scott: It would be fun to hang out in their natural selection style.

480
00:41:19,977 --> 00:41:20,560
Scott: Really sad.

481
00:41:21,340 --> 00:41:27,780
Scott: Really opposite of sad is how amazing that environment is, but also some of the specific things they've put in there.

482
00:41:28,624 --> 00:41:31,320
Scott: The biggest one being the fan art gallery.

483
00:41:32,021 --> 00:41:32,445
Rym: Yes!

484
00:41:32,465 --> 00:41:32,687
Rym: Yes!

485
00:41:32,707 --> 00:41:33,595
Rym: The fan art gallery is brilliant.

486
00:41:35,280 --> 00:41:37,999
Scott: There is no beating around the bush, this is a furry game.

487
00:41:38,620 --> 00:41:41,219
Scott: All the characters are fur people, deal with it.

488
00:41:42,341 --> 00:41:51,740
Scott: In the basement of one of the buildings, in the game, in the lobby, they constructed a gallery of all these fan arts that they've gotten.

489
00:41:51,920 --> 00:41:54,871
Scott: And you can go down there and it's just in the game itself.

490
00:41:54,951 --> 00:41:57,520
Scott: It's not like on their website or on their social media.

491
00:41:58,461 --> 00:42:07,160
Scott: And if you send them fan arts, I've got to assume that they will expand that area and include more fan arts in the game itself.

492
00:42:08,020 --> 00:42:16,300
Scott: So you can go down there and it's actually quite amazing, you know, this gallery space that's built into the game.

493
00:42:17,220 --> 00:42:17,923
Scott: Fantastic.

494
00:42:17,943 --> 00:42:21,113
Scott: I've never seen other games do that to that extent.

495
00:42:22,659 --> 00:42:23,040
Scott: Incredible.

496
00:42:23,800 --> 00:42:29,400
Rym: The characters also add something to the game in that they all have very different cool designs.

497
00:42:30,200 --> 00:42:32,107
Rym: But each one of them has a default power.

498
00:42:32,127 --> 00:42:34,355
Rym: It's almost like a card they always have.

499
00:42:34,375 --> 00:42:35,720
Rym: that always gets played every round.

500
00:42:36,361 --> 00:42:39,220
Rym: So you can have a little bit of control over what you start with.

501
00:42:39,580 --> 00:42:42,050
Rym: I've been using the bird guy who always starts with the little machine gun.

502
00:42:42,070 --> 00:42:43,978
Rym: That's my main guy right now.

503
00:42:46,541 --> 00:42:52,780
Rym: But there's also a ton of cosmetic stuff you can do to the characters that I just haven't engaged with yet, but I feel like I will.

504
00:42:53,220 --> 00:42:55,859
Rym: And it seems like people are into this game really engaged with that.

505
00:42:58,040 --> 00:43:01,153
Rym: And there's one specific thing that stood out that I noticed.

506
00:43:01,173 --> 00:43:02,920
Rym: There's a few ways to do the multiplayer.

507
00:43:03,821 --> 00:43:05,027
Rym: It has a pretty good matchmaking.

508
00:43:05,048 --> 00:43:05,992
Rym: that just happens.

509
00:43:06,053 --> 00:43:07,460
Rym: You can just play with strangers.

510
00:43:08,022 --> 00:43:14,040
Rym: But if you want to make a password protected lobby, like in normal games, what do you do?

511
00:43:14,120 --> 00:43:14,924
Rym: You make the lobby.

512
00:43:14,944 --> 00:43:18,460
Rym: You either invite your friends directly through Steam or through the game.

513
00:43:19,041 --> 00:43:23,320
Rym: Or you put a password on your named lobby and tell your friends, like, come into it.

514
00:43:23,620 --> 00:43:27,418
Rym: The way this one works, I noticed the button just says "Join or Create

515
00:43:27,459 --> 00:43:27,720
Rym: Game."

516
00:43:28,284 --> 00:43:30,680
Rym: And you can type a string of at least six characters.

517
00:43:31,402 --> 00:43:32,524
Rym: You can type any string.

518
00:43:32,544 --> 00:43:39,120
Rym: And if no one is playing a game with that string, you make it.

519
00:43:41,440 --> 00:43:50,580
Rym: So if you type, I don't know, a really funny word, and someone else has also typed that really funny word, you're in a game together now.

520
00:43:52,181 --> 00:43:53,385
Rym: It's not quite a password.

521
00:43:53,426 --> 00:43:57,720
Rym: It's something new that I have not seen this pattern in online games before.

522
00:43:58,101 --> 00:43:59,644
Rym: And I think it's a really good pattern.

523
00:43:59,724 --> 00:44:06,500
Rym: And I hope there are people out there who are making lobbies on words just for fun or trying to find people.

524
00:44:07,641 --> 00:44:08,043
Rym: I don't know.

525
00:44:08,063 --> 00:44:12,360
Rym: I want to see the emergent behavior from this design pattern specifically.

526
00:44:18,181 --> 00:44:18,341
Scott: It's $2.49.

527
00:44:18,341 --> 00:44:22,014
Rym: If you listen to GeekNights, buy this game.

528
00:44:24,061 --> 00:44:26,508
Scott: With a whole bunch of DLCs in a bundle.

529
00:44:27,250 --> 00:44:30,038
Scott: And the DLCs, I think, are just cosmetic, but who cares?

530
00:44:30,058 --> 00:44:30,640
Scott: They're so cheap.

531
00:44:31,901 --> 00:44:34,146
Scott: And they're never going to do any pay-to-wins, ever.

532
00:44:34,868 --> 00:44:38,397
Scott: It's just time grinding, and even not that much of that, really.

533
00:44:38,778 --> 00:44:39,720
Scott: It doesn't matter that much.

534
00:44:40,120 --> 00:44:58,700
Scott: So if you want to play something with a unique concept, with amazing arts and atmosphere to sort of make up for any suffering you have from losing a bunch, or maybe you want to really get good at it and have high skills in quick matches, or maybe you don't want high skills, but you have enough friends.

535
00:44:58,880 --> 00:45:00,023
Scott: You don't need that many friends.

536
00:45:00,063 --> 00:45:03,433
Scott: You just need one friend is enough to play this game.

537
00:45:03,453 --> 00:45:06,160
Scott: You can play 1v1, and that's a full experience.

538
00:45:06,641 --> 00:45:08,244
Rym: And they're really different.

539
00:45:08,485 --> 00:45:10,590
Rym: 1v1 is like a really tense duel.

540
00:45:10,610 --> 00:45:14,820
Rym: You know that feeling we used to get at RIT when you're playing old-school Counter-Strike?

541
00:45:15,201 --> 00:45:20,153
Rym: And there's just one person alive on each team, and that tense, sneaking-around standoff.

542
00:45:20,634 --> 00:45:23,020
Rym: You get that feeling a lot in this game.

543
00:45:23,660 --> 00:45:27,313
Scott: Normally, a 1v1 FPS is just like, "Who sees the other guy

544
00:45:27,353 --> 00:45:28,216
Scott: first?".

545
00:45:28,698 --> 00:45:29,220
Scott: And that's it.

546
00:45:29,500 --> 00:45:36,937
Scott: And so, by adding the cards, the 1v1 actually becomes this wild game of luck and strategy.

547
00:45:37,017 --> 00:45:38,260
Scott: and what cards did you bring.

548
00:45:38,480 --> 00:45:43,211
Scott: And you see how they're trying to play, what character they've chosen.

549
00:45:43,652 --> 00:45:47,340
Scott: Like, if they choose Tiger-Melee-Only-Katana-Guy, right?

550
00:45:47,420 --> 00:45:48,667
Scott: Yes, Tiger with a katana.

551
00:45:48,687 --> 00:45:50,357
Scott: If that doesn't sell the game, I don't know what will.

552
00:45:51,901 --> 00:45:56,733
Scott: If you know they're the Tiger with the katana, you can change your strategy.

553
00:45:57,394 --> 00:45:59,620
Scott: So they're probably doing things to disable your guns.

554
00:46:00,661 --> 00:46:07,419
Scott: You can do things to sort of keep them away from you, or to make yourself move faster, so they can't get within range to use the katana on you.

555
00:46:08,001 --> 00:46:09,587
Scott: And shoot them while you're running away.

556
00:46:09,607 --> 00:46:12,817
Scott: You have to think about that sort of thing in these matches.

557
00:46:16,262 --> 00:46:23,160
Scott: The FPS skill matters significantly enough that I suck, but it's not the only thing that matters.

558
00:46:23,280 --> 00:46:29,180
Scott: The other things will get you some kills here and there, and if you combine both, now you're ready for the big leagues.

559
00:46:34,690 --> 00:46:37,060
Rym: This has been GeekNights with Rym and Scott.

560
00:46:37,120 --> 00:46:39,630
Rym: Special thanks to DJ Pretzel for the opening theme.

561
00:46:39,810 --> 00:46:45,850
Scott: Be sure to visit our website at FrontRowClue.com for show notes, discussion, links, and more.

562
00:46:46,330 --> 00:46:49,022
Rym: Remember, GeekNights is not one, but four different shows.

563
00:46:49,323 --> 00:46:50,890
Rym: Mondays are science and technology.

564
00:46:51,030 --> 00:46:52,755
Rym: Tuesdays, gaming and games.

565
00:46:52,876 --> 00:46:54,701
Rym: Wednesdays are animation and comics.

566
00:46:55,022 --> 00:46:57,510
Rym: Thursdays are arbitrary and indiscriminate.

567
00:46:58,190 --> 00:47:03,030
Rym: GeekNights is distributed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.

568
00:47:03,531 --> 00:47:07,881
Scott: GeekNights is recorded live with no studio and no audience.

569
00:47:08,362 --> 00:47:11,830
Scott: But unlike those other late shows, it's actually recorded at night.

570
00:47:34,331 --> 00:47:35,394
Rym: So yeah, we're back.

571
00:47:35,855 --> 00:47:38,242
Rym: I'm going to continue that series, as I suggested.

572
00:47:38,262 --> 00:47:41,170
Rym: I'm trying to finish it for like two a month, I think.

573
00:47:41,590 --> 00:47:45,370
Rym: And I'll just pick whichever one of the previous topics got the most engagement and go a little deeper.

574
00:47:46,131 --> 00:47:47,413
Rym: And it'll be a fun little aside.

575
00:47:48,415 --> 00:47:56,674
Rym: Otherwise, tonight, on this September 17th, I leave you with... These aren't cattails.

576
00:47:58,298 --> 00:48:02,570
Rym: They're just fairly common water reads and they're not edible.

577
00:48:04,840 --> 00:48:07,445
Rym: But don't you think they ought to be?

578
00:48:07,465 --> 00:48:15,880
Rym: They've got stalks and these groupings of highly compressed seedlings that expand rapidly on impact.

579
00:48:16,562 --> 00:48:18,829
Rym: So they're not that good for eating.

580
00:48:21,056 --> 00:48:21,818
Rym: But even so...

581
00:48:23,900 --> 00:48:24,902
Scott: Me want bite.

582
00:48:24,922 --> 00:48:29,470
Scott: Me want plant, corn, dog, delight.

583
00:48:29,490 --> 00:48:31,533
Rym: Me want deep fried.

584
00:48:33,156 --> 00:48:35,580
Rym: Me think water, Twinkie, nice.

